#. type: SH
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:3
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:5
msgid "sysstat - sysstat configuration file."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:12
msgid ""
"This file is read by B<sa1>(8) and B<sa2>(8) shell scripts from the "
"sysstat's set of tools.  It consists of a sequence of shell variable "
"assignments used to configure sysstat logging.  The variables and their "
"meanings are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<COMPRESSAFTER>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:17
msgid ""
"Number of days after which daily data files are to be compressed.  The "
"compression program is given in the B<ZIP >variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<DELAY_RANGE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:25
msgid ""
"Tell B<sa2> script to wait for a random delay in the indicated range before "
"running.  This delay is expressed in seconds, and is aimed at preventing a "
"massive I/O burst at the same time on VM sharing the same storage area.  A "
"value of 0 means that B<sa2> script will generate its reports files "
"immediately."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<HISTORY>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:35
msgid ""
"The number of days during which a daily data file or a report should be "
"kept. Data files or reports older than this number of days will be removed "
"by the B<sa2>(8) shell script.  Data files and reports are normally saved in "
"the /var/log/sa directory, under the name I<saDD >(for data files) or "
"I<sarDD >(for reports), where the I<DD> parameter indicates the current day."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:51
msgid ""
"The number of files actually kept in the /var/log/sa directory may be "
"slightly higher than the B<HISTORY >value due to the way the B<sa2> script "
"figures out which files are to be removed (see below \"How the B<sa2>(8) "
"script applies B<HISTORY> value\"). Using a value of 28 keeps a whole "
"month's worth of data. If you set B<HISTORY> to a value greater than 28 then "
"you should consider using B<sadc>'s option B<-D> to prevent older data files "
"from being overwritten (see B<sadc>(8)  manual page). In this latter case "
"data files are named I<saYYYYMMDD >and reports I<sarYYYYMMDD>, where I<YYYY "
">stands for the current year, I<MM >for the current month and I<DD> for the "
"current day."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:54
msgid "How the B<sa2>(8) script applies B<HISTORY >value"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:67
msgid ""
"The B<sa2> script uses the B<find >command with the B<-mtime >option to "
"figure out which files are to be removed. The B<find >command interprets "
"this value as \"N 24 hour periods\", ignoring any fractional part. This "
"means that the last modified time of a given I<sa[r]DD >data or report file, "
"using a B<HISTORY> of 1, has to have been modified at least two days ago "
"before it will be removed. And for a B<HISTORY >of 28 that would mean 29 "
"days ago."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:82
msgid ""
"To figure out how a B<HISTORY> of 28 is applied in practice, we need to "
"consider that the B<sa2 >script that issues the B<find >command to remove "
"the old files typically runs just before midnight on a given system, and "
"since the first record from B<sadc> can also be written to the previous "
"day's data file (thereby moving its modification time up a bit), the B<sa2> "
"script will leave 30 files untouched. So for a setting of 28, and counting "
"the data file of the current day, there will always be 31 files (or 30 "
"files, depending on the number of days in a month) in the /var/log/sa "
"directory during the majority of a given day.  E.g.:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:84
msgid "April 30th: 31 files (Apr 30th-1st, Mar 31th)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:86
msgid "May 1st: 30 files (May 1st, Apr 30th-2nd)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:88
msgid ""
"Yet we can note the following exceptions (as inspected at Noon of the given "
"day):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:90
msgid "February 28th: 31 files (Feb 28th-1st, Jan 31st, 30th & 29th)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:92
msgid "March 1st: 30 files (Mar 1st, Feb 28th-2nd, Jan 31st & 30th)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:94
msgid "March 2nd: 29 files (Mar 1st & 2nd, Feb 28th-3rd, Jan. 31st)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:96
msgid "March 3rd: 28 files (Mar 1st-3rd, Feb 28th-4th)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:98
msgid "March 4th - March 28th: 28 files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:100
msgid "March 29th: 29 files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:102
msgid "March 30th: 30 files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:104
msgid "March 31st: 31 files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:107
msgid ""
"(Determining the number of files in March on a leap year is left as an "
"exercise for the reader)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:115
msgid ""
"Things are simpler if you use the I<sa[r]YYYYMMDD >name format.  Apply the "
"same logic as above in this case and you will find that there are always "
"B<HISTORY >+ 3 files in the I</var/log/sa> directory during the majority of "
"a given day."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REPEAT_HEADER>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:121
msgid ""
"Maximum number of lines after which a header will be inserted in the report "
"generated by B<sa2> script. By default there is only a header at the "
"beginning of each report and it is not repeated afterwards."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REPORTS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:127
msgid ""
"Set this variable to B<false >to prevent the B<sa2> script from generating "
"reports (the I<sarDD >files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SA_DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:132
msgid ""
"Directory where the standard system activity daily data and report files are "
"saved. Its default value is I</var/log/sa>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SADC_OPTIONS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:142
msgid ""
"Options that should be passed to B<sadc>(8).  With these options (see "
"B<sadc>(8)  manual page), you can select some additional data which are "
"going to be saved in daily data files.  These options are used only when a "
"new data file is created. They will be ignored with an already existing one."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<UMASK>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:151
msgid ""
"The B<sa1> and B<sa2> scripts generate system activity data and report files "
"in the I</var/log/sa> directory. By default the files are created with umask "
"0022 and are therefore readable for all users. Change this variable to "
"restrict the permissions on the files (e.g. use 0027 to adhere to more "
"strict security standards)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<YESTERDAY>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:161
msgid ""
"By default B<sa2> script generates yesterday's summary, since the B<cron "
">job usually runs right after midnight. If you want B<sa2> to generate the "
"summary of the same day (for example when cron job runs at 23:53) set this "
"variable to B<no>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ZIP>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man5/sysstat.5:164
msgid "Program used to compress data and report files."
msgstr ""

